  "rules": [
    "Model exhibits severe underestimation during high-volatility regimes; when recent history shows a spike (e.g., >80) followed by consolidation, the forecast fails to capture the subsequent breakout and instead predicts a slow, linear drift.",
    "Forecast horizon is systematically biased downward relative to true outcomes; actual price paths often accelerate upward within the first 5-7 days of the forecast window, whereas model predictions remain flat or incrementally rising.",
    "Sentiment signals (0.20-0.50) appear insufficient to trigger upward revisions in the model; even with positive sentiment history, the forecast fails to account for momentum generated by recent price spikes.",
    "Mean reversion is misinterpreted as continuation; when true outcomes show a sharp rise followed by volatility contraction, the model incorrectly projects a steady climb rather than a volatile range-bound path.",
    "Lag effect in reaction to historical peaks: if the last 3 days include a high value (e.g., >81), the model ignores this as a new baseline and continues projecting from lower recent averages."
  ]
}
